Subject:    ggml-cuda: stop direct ROCm_Host compute on HIP integrated GPUs, so
            multi-sequence batches on gfx1151 no longer return corrupted output
Upstream:   ggml-org/llama.cpp#25863 -- OPEN, and NOT OURS. We filed nothing and
            will not (standing project ruling on outward submissions). It is
            authored by Victor-Loos, reviewed and independently validated by an
            AMD engineer (liminfei-amd), who asked for merge on 2026-07-24. It
            was still unmerged when this patch was carried.
            BECAUSE IT MAY MERGE, THE GUARD'S FAILURE MESSAGE HAS TWO ARMS.
            See "TWO OUTCOMES" below -- this is NOT one of our permanent carries.
Applies to: TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant @ fb2cc35ab3507a5aab56fe593620cd96d8f45384
            (ggml/src/ggml-cuda/ggml-cuda.cu, functions
            ggml_backend_cuda_device_supports_buft,
            ggml_backend_cuda_device_get_props,
            ggml_backend_cuda_device_get_host_buffer_type)

WHAT IT FIXES
=============
On gfx1151 (Strix Halo) our HIP build returns WRONG OUTPUT whenever a batch
carries more than one sequence. Measured on Loaf at this pin, gemma-4-31B-it-qat
UD-Q4_K_XL over GPL-3, 14 chunks, four arms:

    CPU reference (-ngl 0, touches no HIP)  ....  519.2226   <- ground truth
    GPU, n_seq=1  (-b 512)                  ....  526.5510   (+1.4%, normal)
    GPU, n_seq=4, HIP_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1     ....  450.2324   (-13.3%, WRONG)
    GPU, n_seq=4, no variable               ....  542607.9074 / 555865.6336

i.e. TWO defects, not one. The unblocked arm is a race -- catastrophic and
nondeterministic (the two runs above disagree with each other). Serializing
kernel launches with HIP_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 removes the race and leaves a
DETERMINISTIC 13-23% error, present at every one of the fourteen chunks, in the
same direction. A stable wrong answer is still a wrong answer, and blocking is
therefore NOT a remedy: it buys reproducibility, not correctness.

ROOT CAUSE, bisected upstream (not by us) to ggml-org/llama.cpp c7d87229
"ggml-cuda : restore prop.integrated on HIP builds" (#24233): restoring the real
prop.integrated on HIP made supports_buft(ROCm_Host) flip false -> true, so the
CUDA backend began computing DIRECTLY on host-visible buffers on APUs. At this
pin the enabling line is still there --

    #if defined(GGML_USE_HIP)
            info.devices[id].integrated = prop.integrated;
    #else
            info.devices[id].integrated = false; // Temporarily disabled ... (#15034)
    #endif

-- and note the shape of it: the guard EXISTS and is on the WRONG BRANCH.
Upstream disabled the path for CUDA *because of corrupted output* and left it
live for HIP, which is the branch gfx1151 takes. We build -DGGML_HIP=ON on an
integrated GPU, so we are exposed by construction, not by luck.

The upstream symptom is the same defect seen from the serving side: with
-np 4 --kv-unified, llama-server returns complete responses belonging to a
DIFFERENT, earlier request (#25992). liminfei-amd reproduced that on gfx1151 --
2 cross-request replays in 96 responses -- and reported 96/96 clean with this
patch applied.

WHY THIS PATCH AND NOT SOMETHING CHEAPER
========================================
Three alternatives were considered and all are worse:

  * HIP_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 -- MEASURED HERE, DOES NOT FIX IT (the 450.2324 arm
    above). It also serializes every kernel launch. Do not reach for it.
  * GGML_CUDA_NO_PINNED=1 -- disables the host-buffer factory outright. That is
    the FIRST version of this very PR, and liminfei-amd measured it at
    pp512 5857 -> 1502 t/s, a 3.9x prefill loss. It additionally aborts at an
    unpatched pin (their retest notes it "no longer aborts" only WITH the patch),
    so it cannot even be used as a probe here.
  * The Vulkan backend, or halo-box/strix-llama.cpp -- that fork carries the
    IDENTICAL regression (same integrated = prop.integrated under HIP, zero
    occurrences of supports_cuda_host_buft), which is why its own CI sets
    HIP_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1; and it implements no turbo KV type, while
    LLAMA_ARG_CACHE_TYPE_V=turbo3 is our shipped default.

COST: liminfei-amd, gfx1151 / ROCm 7.2.1, exact-parent/head ABBA across
ngl=0,10,99 -- within -1.84% to +0.87%. Pinned staging and output buffers REMAIN
AVAILABLE; only direct compute on ROCm_Host is refused. An independent
llama-bench (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B) put pp512 at 1165.42 -> 1144.09 and tg128 at
67.57 -> 68.11. Those are AMD's numbers on ROCm 7.2.1, not ours on TheRock
7.13.0a20260501 -- re-measure on our stack rather than quoting them forward.

TWO OUTCOMES, AND THEY CALL FOR OPPOSITE ACTIONS
================================================
Unlike our other carried patches, this one has a live upstream PR that an AMD
engineer has asked to merge. When the guard goes red, READ THE SOURCE FIRST:

  (a) supports_cuda_host_buft (or an equivalent) is ALREADY in the pinned tree,
      or the HIP branch no longer sets integrated = prop.integrated.
      => upstream fixed it. DELETE this patch and its guard, advance the pin.
      Do NOT re-derive a hunk for a fix that is already there.
  (b) the stock one-line supports_buft return is STILL present and the hunk
      merely failed to apply. => ordinary drift: RE-DERIVE at the new sha and
      update the guard's counts to match what you actually found.

DEVIATION FROM THE UPSTREAM DIFF: the PR's diff carries "old mode 100644 /
new mode 100755" -- an artifact of the author's checkout. Those two lines are
STRIPPED here; making a source file executable is not part of the fix. Nothing
else differs, so a re-derivation can diff this body against the PR directly.

